5234375, 0.00292205810546875, 0.0043487548828125, 0.0084075927734375, 0.0017948150634765625, 0.00853729248046875, -0.027191162109375, -0.005512237548828125, 0.02484130859375, 0.0108184814453125, 0.00907135009765625, -0.0170440673828125, 0.0037555694580078125, -0.018463134765625, 0.020965576171875, -0.009796142578125, 0.003360748291015625, -0.0116424560546875, -0.01346588134765625, 0.024444580078125, -0.007358551025390625, 0.017303466796875, -0.01016998291015625, 0.024658203125, 0.035247802734375, 0.00418853759765625, -0.01105499267578125, -0.006580352783203125, 0.01885986328125, -0.00888824462890625, -0.005344390869140625, 0.00433349609375, -0.0226287841796875, 0.0130157470703125, -0.01806640625, -0.01522064208984375, -0.002346038818359375, -0.018280029296875, 0.0254058837890625, -0.049468994140625, 0.002288818359375, -0.00519561767578125, -0.0194549560546875, 0.0212249755859375, -0.048675537109375, -0.002483367919921875, -0.0177764892578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "# Introduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability of these methods, defined here as the consistency and robustness of agent performance across different runs, task orders, and environmental stochasticity, has been critically overlooked.\n\nWe distinguish reliability and fragility as follows:\n\n- **Reliability** is the degree to which an agent\u2019s performance remains stable and repeatable under variations in experimental conditions (e.g., random seeds, task order, environment changes). High reliability means the agent consistently performs well across such variations.\n- **Fragility** is not simply the inverse of reliability; rather, it refers to the tendency of an agent\u2019s performance to degrade sharply or unpredictably under these variations. Fragility captures not only inconsistency but also the presence of specific vulnerabilities\u2014such as catastrophic failure in certain task orders or environments\u2014that may not be apparent from average performance alone.\n\nFor example, a reliable agent would achieve similar success rates regardless of task order or random seed, whereas a fragile agent might perform well on a fixed task sequence but fail dramatically when the order is shuffled or when minor environment changes occur. Table 1 below summarizes the distinction:\n\n| Term        | Definition                                                                 | Example                                                                                  |\n|-------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Reliability | Stable, repeatable performance across seeds, task orders, and environments | Agent achieves similar success rates regardless of task order or random seed              |\n| Fragility   | Susceptibility to sharp or unpredictable performance degradation under variations in experimental conditions | Agent performs well on a fixed task sequence but fails when order is shuffled or perturbed|\n\nTo further clarify, consider a navigation agent that learns to traverse a virtual map: if it reliably succeeds whether it learns the rooms in order A\u2192B\u2192C or C\u2192B\u2192A, it is reliable; if it only succeeds in one order, it is fragile.\n\nIn this work, we address the following research questions:\n1. How robust are memory-based self-improving agents to stochasticity and task order?\n2. What factors contribute to their observed fragility, and how can evaluation protocols be improved?\n\nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods: Episodic Memory Augmented Agents (EMAA) and Continual Prompt Memory (CPM). These methods were selected based on the following criteria: (a) they exemplify two widely used paradigms in the literature\u2014episodic memory augmentation and continual prompt-based memory; (b) they have open-source implementations and are commonly adopted as baselines in recent studies; and (c) they implement explicit memory storage and retrieval mechanisms, which are central to our research questions. While these choices allow for controlled and interpretable comparisons, we acknowledge that restricting our study to EMAA and CPM may introduce bias and limit generalizability to agents with fundamentally different memory architectures or learning dynamics, such as those using implicit or non-textual memory. This limitation means our findings may not extend to all memory-based agents, especially those with alternative architectures. To partially mitigate this, we selected two methods that are widely used and represent distinct paradigms, but we did not include agents with fundamentally different memory mechanisms. As such, our results should be interpreted as indicative for this class of agents, and future work should expand the scope to include a broader range of architectures.\n\n# Methods\n\n## Experimental Setup\n\nOur methodology broadens the scope of evaluation along two axes. First, we include multiple runs (five per configuration, each with a different random seed) to quantify variance. Second, we randomly shuffle the order of tasks to investigate the effect of task order. Specifically, for each configuration, we generate five random permutations of the task sequence using a uniform random shuffle; due to combinatorial explosion, we do not exhaustively enumerate all possible orderings. This sampling approach provides a tractable yet diverse set of orderings, but we acknowledge that it may not capture the full spectrum of possible task dependencies or worst-case orderings. Rare or adversarial orderings may be missed, which could lead to underestimating the true extent of fragility. We considered alternative sampling strategies, such as targeted adversarial orderings or increasing the number of permutations, but were constrained by computational resources. To assess whether five permutations and seeds are statistically adequate, we conducted a post-hoc power analysis: with observed effect sizes (Cohen\u2019s d > 1.0) for order sensitivity, five samples per group yield >80% power to detect significant differences at \u03b1=0.05. Nevertheless, our conclusions about order sensitivity are limited to the sampled permutations, and future work should explore more exhaustive or targeted sampling to better characterize worst-case fragility.\n\n### Environment Details\n\nExperiments are conducted on two complex multi-step environments:\n\n- **Text-based navigation domain:**  \n  - 15 tasks requiring the agent to navigate a virtual space using natural language commands (e.g., \u201cGo north to the library, then east to the kitchen\u201d).\n  - Each task specifies a start and goal location, possible paths, and distractor objects (e.g., \u201cYou see a red chair\u201d in a room not relevant to the goal).\n  - Distractors are implemented as irrelevant objects or misleading textual descriptions.\n  - Task difficulty is determined by minimum navigation steps (easy: 2\u20133, medium: 4\u20136, hard: 7+), number of distractors (few vs. many), and whether prior knowledge (e.g., map layouts) is required.\n\n- **Procedural puzzle-solving suite:**  \n  - 12 tasks involving multi-step logical reasoning to solve puzzles such as code-breaking (\u201cFind the 3-digit code using the clues provided\u201d) or sequence prediction.\n  - Each task defines a goal state, a sequence of intermediate steps, and a set of clues, some of which are distractors (extraneous clues or misleading intermediate states).\n  - Difficulty is categorized by number of reasoning steps (easy: 2\u20133, medium: 4\u20135, hard: 6+), dependencies on earlier tasks, and distractor complexity.\n\nFor further details and reproducibility, we provide references to the open-source implementations and environment generators used in our experiments (see Supplementary Materials).\n\n## Evaluation Metrics\n\nWe evaluate agents using three primary metrics:\n\n- **Success rate:** Percentage of tasks completed successfully, reflecting overall task competence.\n- **Average episode length:** Number of steps taken per episode, indicating efficiency and potential confusion.\n- **Memory utilization:** Number of relevant memory entries retrieved per task, measuring the agent\u2019s reliance on and use of its memory.\n\nThese metrics were chosen to capture both effectiveness (success rate), efficiency (episode length), and the core mechanism under study (memory utilization). We considered but did not include learning speed and generalization to unseen tasks. Learning speed was excluded because all agents were trained under fixed training budgets, making speed differences less informative for robustness assessment. Generalization to unseen tasks, while important, was left for future work as our focus is on robustness within the defined task suite. We acknowledge that omitting these metrics may bias our assessment by potentially overlooking agents that are robust but slow to learn, or those that generalize well but are less robust in-distribution. While we did not include proxy measures in this study, future work should incorporate metrics for learning speed (e.g., episodes to convergence) and generalization (e.g., performance on held-out tasks) to provide a more comprehensive assessment.\n\n## Memory Analysis\n\nTo better understand agent fragility, we manually examine the agents\u2019 memory by reviewing stored trajectories and decision rationales. For each agent and environment, we randomly sampled 40 memory entries (20 from default order runs, 20 from shuffled order runs), stratified across task difficulty levels (easy, medium, hard) to ensure representativeness.\n\nThe rubric for analysis was developed through an iterative process: initial criteria were piloted on a subset of 10 entries, after which annotators discussed discrepancies and ambiguous cases. Based on these discussions, the rubric was refined to include the following criteria:\n\n- **Completeness:** Does the entry capture all information necessary to solve the task? (1: major omissions, 2: partial, 3: fully complete)\n- **Relevance:** Is the stored information pertinent to future tasks? (1: irrelevant, 2: somewhat relevant, 3: highly relevant)\n- **Consistency:** Are memory entries internally coherent and non-contradictory? (1: contradictory, 2: minor inconsistencies, 3: fully consistent)\n\nTwo independent annotators scored each memory entry on this 3-point scale per criterion. Inter-annotator agreement, measured by Cohen\u2019s kappa, averaged 0.81, indicating substantial agreement. Disagreements were resolved through joint review and consensus discussion. While the sample size is limited and may not capture rare or edge-case memory behaviors, stratified random sampling across tasks and difficulties increases the likelihood that the analyzed entries are representative of overall agent memory behavior. Nonetheless, the small sample size may limit the generalizability of qualitative findings, and future work should expand the analysis to larger and more diverse samples.\n\n## Statistical Analysis\n\nStatistical significance of differences between default and shuffled orders was assessed using two-sided t-tests. To address the issue of multiple comparisons (e.g., across tasks and metrics), we applied the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure to control the false discovery rate at 0.05. Assumptions of normality were checked using the Shapiro-Wilk test for each group; in cases where normality was violated, we confirmed results using the non-parametric Mann-Whitney U test, which yielded consistent conclusions. Effect sizes were calculated using Cohen\u2019s d for all key metrics. For each metric, we report 95% confidence intervals (CIs) and effect sizes to provide a transparent and consistent summary of statistical results.\n\n# Results\n\nWe present quantitative results in Table 2, summarizing agent performance across environments, task orders, and seeds. Each configuration reflects five independent runs (random seeds)"}}